diagnosis_text:
I validated end-to-end reachability and the routed fabric. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 (and back), pings succeed with 0% loss and ~1.06–1.07 ms RTT, so host-to-host traffic is currently flowing. On the leafs, the remote rack subnet is installed and reachable: leaf_router_0_0 has B>* 10.0.1.0/24 via 172.16.0.4 (eth0), and leaf_router_0_1 has B>* 10.0.0.0/24 via 172.16.0.6 (eth0). Both uplinks on each leaf are up (connected 172.16.0.4/31 & 0.8/31 on leaf_router_0_0; 172.16.0.6/31 & 0.10/31 on leaf_router_0_1). 

The BGP control plane is healthy across the CLOS: all sessions are Established on both leaves, both spines, and the super-spine. Each leaf receives prefixes from both spines (PfxRcd 1 from each). Spines and the super-spine likewise show stable adjacencies with prefixes exchanged. This means there is no current blackholing or “dead path” in use. Note the Up/Down timers (~1:45) indicate a recent convergence (e.g., lab start), but the fabric is now fully converged.

Root cause: No fault present at this time; the network is operating normally and delivering traffic between racks.

Corrective fix: None required. If the issue reappears intermittently, capture timestamps and a traceroute from the affected host to correlate. Optionally, enable BGP multipath on the leafs/spines to use both spine paths concurrently for additional resiliency, but this is a design enhancement, not a fix for a current fault.
